.TH TELETE 1 "October 18, 2026" "Telete 0.1.1" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
telete \- run a serial queue of committed repository workflows
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B telete \-\-config
.I file
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B telete
.RB { \-h | \-\-help | \-V | \-\-version }
.br
.B telete help
.RI [ command " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B telete
runs committed source from a local Git repository on macOS.
A project JSON configuration selects the repository, initial private runtime
directory, required check command, optional preparation and deployment commands,
and parameters.
Each submitted job runs one exact commit in a private checkout.
Jobs run serially, in queue order.
.PP
Submission resolves the requested revision once and freezes the workflow and
parameters with the job.
The default revision is HEAD in the configured repository at submission time.
Uncommitted changes are not included.
Later changes to HEAD or the configuration do not alter a submitted job.
Telete runs the selected commit directly; it does not merge source or update
project branches.
.PP
Submission queues work; a separately started foreground worker executes it.
No initialization command or pre-existing runtime directory is required.
Mutating commands create private runtime state on demand.
Every operation must select a project configuration, including when inspecting
an existing queue.
Help and version requests do not require a configuration.
.PP
Creating a development worktree binds the Git common directory to one queue.
The original checkout, every linked worktree, and alternate configurations then
share that queue, including worktrees created directly with Git.
Separate clones retain independent queues.
.SH INSTALLATION
From this repository, run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&./ci/install.sh
.fi
.RE
.PP
The script builds the release binary with Cargo's locked dependency versions,
then installs
.I ~/.local/bin/telete
and
.IR ~/.local/share/man/man1/telete.1 .
It replaces the executable atomically after a successful build.
Use
.B ./ci/install.sh \-\-prefix
.I path
to select another installation prefix.
The script does not edit shell profiles.
.PP
Add the following to your shell profile once, then open a new shell or run the
line in your current shell:
.PP
.RS
.nf
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The command is then available from any directory.
Project operations still require
.BR \-\-config ;
use an absolute configuration path when invoking from another directory.
If the installed manual is not found, run
.BR "man \-M \(dq$HOME/.local/share/man\(dq telete" .
.PP
This repository's
.B ci.sh
wrapper selects
.BR ci/workflow.json .
Its workflow runs
.B ci/check.sh
and then
.B ci/install.sh
from the submitted commit, installing only after checks succeed.
Restart running workers to use an upgraded executable.
See RECOVERY for runtime version compatibility.
.SS Release versions
.B Cargo.toml
owns the package version displayed by
.BR "telete \-\-version" .
Before committing a release, run
.B ./ci/bump-version.sh
to bump the patch version, or pass
.B minor
or
.BR major .
Minor and major bumps reset lower version components.
The helper updates the package manifest, the package's lockfile entry, and this
manual's version header together.
Review and commit these changes before submitting the workflow.
Deployment installs the version in the submitted commit without bumping it.
.SH OPTIONS
The following global options may appear before or after a subcommand.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Read the required project JSON configuration.
A relative configuration path resolves against the current directory.
There is no default configuration path or environment variable selector.
The configuration and configured repository must remain available for queue
operations.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write compact JSON results to standard output and structured operation errors
to standard error.
Without this option, results are pretty-printed JSON and operation errors begin
with
.BR "telete:" .
Command-line parsing errors and help remain ordinary text in either mode.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help for the selected command and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display the program version and exit.
Available at the top level.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "worktree " path
Resolve HEAD once in the configured repository and create a new branch and
Git worktree at that commit.
A relative destination resolves against the invocation's current directory.
The branch name is the normalized destination's last path component, which
must be a valid literal Git branch name.
An existing destination or branch is an error; no existing worktree is adopted.
Uncommitted changes remain in the source checkout.
This command neither submits work nor starts a worker.
The result includes the absolute
.BR path ,
new
.BR branch ,
full
.BR commit ,
and shared runtime
.BR state .
See SHARED QUEUE for queue binding and failure behavior.
.TP
.B submit \fR[\fIrevision\fR]
Queue a new job for a Git revision that resolves to a local commit.
The default is HEAD.
The result includes the job's
.B id
and full
.BR commit .
Each submission creates a separate job, even for a previously submitted commit.
Before reporting success, Telete retains the commit and creates its private
execution checkout.
Removing the submitting linked worktree does not change this retained source;
the shared Git repository and runtime must remain available.
It does not start a worker or wait for completion.
.TP
.B status \fR[\fIjob\fR]
Display the queue, active job, retained failure, settled-job markers, operator
pause, and maintenance owners.
With a job ID, display only that job's retained result.
An unavailable or pruned job is an operation error.
.TP
.BI "wait " "job " "\fR[\fB\-\-timeout \fIseconds\fR]"
Observe a job until it succeeds, fails, is cancelled, becomes blocked, or the
observation times out.
The timeout is a positive integer number of seconds; the default is 60.
This timeout limits observation, not execution.
The command does not advance work or cancel the job.
The result has
.B observation
set to
.B terminal
or
.B timeout
and a
.B job
object containing the observed state.
A blocked job is reported as a terminal observation but remains unresolved.
Inspect the result to determine workflow success; see EXIT STATUS.
.TP
.B pause
Prevent the worker from claiming new jobs.
Submissions are still accepted, and an already active job can continue.
Pausing does not stop the worker or drain the queue.
.TP
.B resume
Clear the operator pause.
Claims remain prevented while any maintenance hold exists.
An unresolved active job must be recovered before resuming.
.TP
.BI "cancel " job
Request cancellation at a safe boundary.
A queued job is cancelled before execution.
For active work, the request is considered before the next command or after
the current command settles.
Cancellation does not interrupt an admitted command, roll back effects, or
resolve uncertain work.
.TP
.BI "recover " job
Reconcile a blocked job using its retained command, context, and terminal
process evidence.
Only blocked jobs can be recovered.
Stop the foreground worker before invoking this command; recovery requires
exclusive worker ownership.
Stopping the worker may leave an admitted command running; wait for its
execution ownership to clear before recovery.
Recovery fails if matching terminal evidence is unavailable.
It does not repeat an uncertain command.
Run the worker afterward to continue remaining work.
.TP
.B worker \fR[\fB\-\-once\fR]
Run the foreground queue worker until stopped or an operation error occurs.
Only one worker can own a queue at a time.
With
.BR \-\-once ,
advance one lifecycle tick and print the resulting job or idle state.
A tick may run a selected stage to completion; it does not necessarily finish
the entire job.
.TP
.BI "maintenance hold \-\-owner " owner
Add a maintenance hold preventing new claims.
Owner names must contain 1 to 256 bytes and no control characters.
Holding an already held owner is harmless.
Multiple owners may hold the queue independently; all holds must be released
before new claims are allowed.
Submissions and active work can continue while a hold exists.
.TP
.B maintenance status
Display maintenance owners and the active job.
The
.B held
field indicates whether any hold exists;
.B drained
means no job is active, even if jobs are queued.
Acquiring a hold does not wait for active work to drain.
.TP
.BI "maintenance release \-\-owner " owner
Release the named hold.
Releasing an absent owner is harmless.
Release only the owner acquired by the maintenance operation.
This command does not clear operator pause or resolve interrupted work.
.TP
.B gates
Inspect retained command execution gate records for current diagnostics.
The broker serializes commands and permits an execution identity to join an
existing result only when its saved command and context match exactly.
Missing terminal evidence blocks its resource slot.
.TP
.B help \fR[\fIcommand\fR ...]
Display general help or help for a subcommand, such as
.BR "telete help maintenance hold" .
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration is a JSON object.
Unknown project and command fields are rejected.
Every operation reads and validates the selected configuration.
.SS Project fields
.TP
.B repo
Required path to the Git repository checkout selecting source.
HEAD is resolved there regardless of the invocation's current directory.
.TP
.B state
Required initial path to the private runtime directory identifying this queue.
Until a shared queue is bound, use the same state directory for queue
operations.
After binding, the recorded shared runtime path takes precedence; changing this
field cannot redirect the queue.
A populated directory is bound to its repository and runtime path and cannot
be reassigned to another repository or moved by changing the configuration.
.TP
.B check
Required command object for checking the selected commit.
.TP
.B prepare
Optional command object, run after a successful check.
Omitted or null means no preparation stage.
.TP
.B deploy
Optional command object, run after all selected preceding stages succeed.
Deployment may be selected without preparation.
Omitted or null means no deployment stage.
.TP
.B parameters
JSON object passed unchanged through the execution context.
The default is an empty object.
.PP
Relative
.B repo
and
.B state
paths resolve against the configuration file's directory.
They are normalized to absolute paths.
Ignore the runtime directory in Git if it is inside the repository.
.SS Command fields
The same command object format applies to all three stages.
.TP
.B program
Required executable path, either absolute or normalized relative to the private
checkout.
Relative paths must stay inside that checkout.
A bare name such as
.B check
means a file in the checkout, not a PATH search.
Paths containing dot or dot-dot components, repeated slashes, or a trailing
slash are rejected; use
.BR ci/check.sh ,
not
.BR ./ci/check.sh .
.TP
.B args
Array of literal string arguments; the default is an empty array.
Telete performs no shell interpretation or variable expansion.
Select a shell explicitly when needed, for example
.B program
set to
.B /bin/sh
with
.B args
set to
.BR "[\(dq\-c\(dq, \(dqexec ci/check.sh\(dq]" .
.TP
.B env
Object of string environment overrides; the default is an empty object.
Names must start with an ASCII letter or underscore and contain only ASCII
letters, digits, and underscores.
Names beginning with
.B TELETE_
are reserved and cannot be overridden.
Values are literal: Telete neither expands them nor resolves paths in them
against the configuration directory.
.TP
.B timeout_seconds
Positive integer execution timeout in seconds.
Omitted or null means no time limit.
On timeout, Telete terminates the command's process group and records failure.
.PP
Programs, arguments, and environment values cannot contain NUL.
.SH SHARED QUEUE
The first
.B worktree
invocation binds Git's common directory to the selected configuration's absolute
.B state
path, recording the private binding at
.IR G/telete/queue.json .
Here
.I G
is the common Git directory shared by the original checkout and linked
worktrees.
Every Telete command selecting one of those checkouts then uses this runtime
directory, including commands using another config or a manually created
worktree.
Separate clones have different Git common directories and independent queues.
.PP
Initial binding requires exclusive repository selection.
If a worker or operation is using an unbound repository, stop existing workers
and retry after the operation finishes.
Once bound, the repository permits worktree creation while its shared worker
runs.
.PP
The selected configuration still chooses the source repository, commands, and
parameters.
Submission freezes each job's own commit, workflow, and parameters before
joining the common queue.
Select the corresponding config in each checkout when its repository path is
relative.
Passing the original checkout's config from inside a child continues to select
the original repository's HEAD.
A copied config's relative state path cannot create a second queue after
binding.
.PP
All linked worktrees share queue order, worker ownership, pause, maintenance
holds, cancellation, recovery, diagnostics, and retention limits.
An unresolved command blocks subsequent work from every checkout.
Telete rejects conflicting existing runtime state instead of combining queues.
.PP
The queue binding can remain if Git worktree creation fails.
Inspect the reported branch and destination before retrying; Telete does not
remove pre-existing branches or worktrees to recover a failed creation.
Preserve the shared Git repository, binding, and runtime directory while work
is queued, active, or unresolved.
If the binding survives but the runtime directory or queue database is missing,
all operations fail and require restoration of that operational state.
Telete does not create an empty replacement queue behind an existing binding.
.SH EXECUTION
Stages run in this order: check, optional preparation, optional deployment.
A stage succeeds only when its command exits with status 0 without a timeout,
signal, start error, or output collection error.
A failed stage stops the job.
An unselected stage is reported as
.BR not_requested ,
not as a successful execution.
The configured commands own their checks, output formats, credentials,
deployment effects, and any stronger claim of domain success.
.PP
All stages use the same private checkout as their working directory.
They inherit the environment at launch, with configured overrides and Telete's
execution context.
The inherited environment is not frozen with the submission or retained.
Commands run with ordinary user permissions.
Configure any required toolchain, caches, credentials, or environment in the
project's commands; Telete imposes no filesystem sandbox or reproducibility
guarantee.
.PP
Standard input is closed.
Standard output and standard error are captured separately, up to 16 MiB each,
and decoded as UTF-8 with replacement for invalid bytes.
Excess output is drained but not retained, and an output collection error makes
the stage fail even if its exit status is zero.
A known terminal exit remains recorded separately from collection errors.
Failure to start a program is recorded as a start error.
Commands must not leave background work: Telete kills surviving processes in
the command's process group after the direct child exits.
.SS Execution context
.B TELETE_CONTEXT
contains the absolute path to a private JSON context file supplied to each
stage.
The context includes:
.TP
.BR id ", " job_id ", " stage
The execution identity, job identity, and stage name.
.TP
.B checkout
The absolute private checkout path.
.TP
.B source.commit
The full commit ID selected at submission.
.TP
.B parameters
The submitted parameters object.
.TP
.B outputs.directory
The private output directory for this command.
.TP
.B inputs.preparation
The absolute output directory of the successful preparation stage, when
available; otherwise null.
.PP
Telete defines no package or installation format for these outputs.
Commands that need durable outputs must copy or publish them before runtime
cleanup.
.SH OUTPUT
Operation results are JSON on standard output.
Operation errors are written to standard error.
Help and version requests print text instead.
Operation results include the effective absolute runtime directory as the
top-level
.B state_root
field.
The foreground worker normally runs without printing progress; inspect it
from another terminal using
.B status
or
.BR wait .
.PP
Job phases are
.BR queued ,
.BR starting ,
.BR checking ,
.BR preparing ,
.BR deploying ,
.BR blocked ,
.BR succeeded ,
.BR failed ,
and
.BR cancelled .
Detailed records include stage results and current execution diagnostics.
Settled jobs may instead have only a small completion marker; see FILES.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested operation completed successfully, or help or version was printed.
This does not establish that a workflow succeeded.
In particular,
.B wait
returns zero for failed, cancelled, and blocked jobs and for observation
timeout; inspect
.B observation
and
.BR job.phase .
.B worker \-\-once
can also return zero while reporting a failed or blocked job.
.TP
.B 1
An operation failed, including a missing configuration selection, invalid
configuration, unavailable job, conflicting ownership, or recovery without
matching terminal evidence.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
the error object has
.B ok
set to false and an
.B error
string.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line syntax, such as an unknown option or missing required
command argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TELETE_CONTEXT
Set by Telete for configured commands; see EXECUTION.
.TP
.B TELETE_*
Reserved for Telete's context; unavailable as configured environment overrides.
.PP
Other environment variables are inherited by configured commands and may be
overridden through their
.B env
objects.
Environment values are not retained unless explicitly configured.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I file
The project JSON configuration selected by
.BR \-\-config .
It is separate from runtime state.
.TP
.I G/telete/queue.json
Private binding from Git's common directory to the shared runtime directory,
created by the first worktree command.
.TP
.I G/telete/queue.lock
Repository selection lock coordinating ordinary queue operations with initial
shared binding.
This lock can exist before the queue binding is established.
.TP
.I state/queue.sqlite3
The operational queue, repository binding, pause state, maintenance holds,
and bounded completion markers in the effective runtime directory.
.TP
.I state/jobs/job/
Private checkout, command requests and contexts, stage results, logs, and
command output directories for a retained job.
.TP
.I state/broker/
Current execution gate records and terminal process evidence.
.TP
.IR state/tmp/ ", " state/deployments/ ", " state/*.lock
Temporary files and execution coordination locks managed by Telete.
.PP
The
.I state
and
.I job
components above stand for the effective runtime path and a job ID.
Runtime directories must be owned by the invoking user with mode 0700.
The
.I G/telete
directory is also private with mode 0700; its binding and lock files use mode
0600.
Symbolic links anywhere in the runtime path are rejected; select a physical
path rather than a macOS path alias.
.PP
.BR status ,
.BR wait ,
.BR gates ,
and
.B maintenance status
do not initialize missing state.
Without a shared binding, a missing runtime directory has no work and permits
new claims.
An existing binding with a missing runtime directory or queue database is an
error requiring restoration of operational state.
.PP
Runtime state is for current operation, not a permanent history.
Worker cleanup releases successful and cancelled jobs' scratch files and full
execution records.
The latest failed workflow retains its diagnostics and checkout until a newer
workflow succeeds or fails; cancellation does not supersede that failure.
Superseded failure details are also removed.
.PP
At most 128 small settled-job markers are retained.
They contain the job ID, phase, queue sequence, and update time, without source,
commands, parameters, environment, or output.
After pruning,
.B status job
and
.B wait job
report that the job is unavailable.
These limits apply to the shared queue as a whole, not separately per worktree.
.SH RECOVERY
An interrupted command without matching terminal evidence remains unresolved
and blocks later work.
Process disappearance does not establish completion.
Telete does not automatically repeat or roll back uncertain effects.
.PP
Preserve the runtime directory while work is active or unresolved.
Deleting or editing evidence can prevent safe reconciliation.
Inspect
.BR "status job" ,
.BR gates ,
and retained diagnostics before stopping the worker and running
.BR "recover job" .
Recovery uses the saved invocation, including its context, explicit environment
overrides, and timeout, rather than current command settings.
It does not authorize another launch under the same execution identity.
.PP
Use the Telete version that owns existing runtime state.
Incompatible runtime schemas are rejected; Telete does not automatically migrate
active queues.
.SH EXAMPLES
Configure a repository with an executable, committed
.B ci/check.sh
and optional preparation and deployment scripts.
Save this file as
.B telete.json
in the repository root, and ignore
.B .telete/
in Git:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "repo": ".",
  "state": ".telete",
  "check": {
    "program": "ci/check.sh",
    "args": [],
    "env": {},
    "timeout_seconds": 600
  },
  "prepare": {"program": "ci/prepare.sh"},
  "deploy": {"program": "ci/deploy.sh"},
  "parameters": {"suite": "all"}
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Submit HEAD and start the worker in the foreground.
Omit the
.B prepare
and
.B deploy
fields for a check-only workflow:
.PP
.RS
.nf
telete \-\-config telete.json submit
telete \-\-config telete.json worker
.fi
.RE
.PP
In another terminal, use the ID returned by submission in place of JOB:
.PP
.RS
.nf
telete \-\-config telete.json status
telete \-\-config telete.json \-\-json wait JOB \-\-timeout 300
.fi
.RE
.PP
Submit a particular revision:
.PP
.RS
.nf
telete \-\-config telete.json submit v1.2.3
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create two branches and linked worktrees, then submit their respective HEADs to
one queue:
.PP
.RS
.nf
telete worktree ../feature-a \-\-config telete.json
telete worktree ../feature-b \-\-config telete.json
telete \-\-config ../feature-a/telete.json submit
telete \-\-config ../feature-b/telete.json submit
telete \-\-config telete.json status
.fi
.RE
.PP
Hold admission for maintenance, inspect
.BR drained ,
and release the same owner when maintenance is complete:
.PP
.RS
.nf
telete \-\-config telete.json maintenance hold \-\-owner upgrade
telete \-\-config telete.json maintenance status
telete \-\-config telete.json maintenance release \-\-owner upgrade
.fi
.RE
.PP
After stopping the worker, inspect and reconcile a blocked job, then continue
execution:
.PP
.RS
.nf
telete \-\-config telete.json status JOB
telete \-\-config telete.json gates
telete \-\-config telete.json recover JOB
telete \-\-config telete.json worker
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR git-worktree (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR gitrevisions (7)
